  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.summary}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "1. Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "2. Scope" },
        { id: "s3", label: "3. The Management Approach — Segments as Management Sees Them" },
        { id: "s4", label: "4. Identifying Reportable Segments — the Quantitative Thresholds" },
        { id: "s5", label: "5. Required Segment-Level Disclosures" },
        { id: "s6", label: "6. Reconciliation to Consolidated Totals" },
        { id: "s7", label: "7. Entity-Wide Disclosures — Products, Geography, and Major Customers" },
        { id: "s8", label: "8. Worked Example — Applying the 10% Reportable Segment Threshold" },
        { id: "s9", label: "9. Worked Example — a Segment Reconciliation" },
        { id: "s10", label: "10. Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s11", label: "11. Critical Judgments" },
        { id: "s12", label: "12. Relationship to IAS/IFRS" },
        { id: "s13", label: "13. Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s14", label: "14. Related Standards" },
        { id: "s15", label: "15. Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <div class="wrap">
<div class="hub">
  <div class="hub-box">
    <div class="hub-title">ASBJ Statement No. 17 — Accounting Standard for Disclosures about Segments of an Enterprise</div>
    <div class="hub-sub">Extended reference poster · Japan GAAP (J-GAAP) · セグメント情報等の開示に関する会計基準 · Genuinely converged with IFRS 8's management approach · JPY examples</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="toc"><a href="#s1">1. Conceptual Framework Linkage</a><a href="#s2">2. Scope</a><a href="#s3">3. The Management Approach — Segments as Management Sees Them</a><a href="#s4">4. Identifying Reportable Segments — the Quantitative Thresholds</a><a href="#s5">5. Required Segment-Level Disclosures</a><a href="#s6">6. Reconciliation to Consolidated Totals</a><a href="#s7">7. Entity-Wide Disclosures — Products, Geography, and Major Customers</a><a href="#s8">8. Worked Example — Applying the 10% Reportable Segment Threshold</a><a href="#s9">9. Worked Example — a Segment Reconciliation</a><a href="#s10">10. Disclosure Requirements</a><a href="#s11">11. Critical Judgments</a><a href="#s12">12. Relationship to IAS/IFRS</a><a href="#s13">13. Statement Mapping</a><a href="#s14">14. Related Standards</a><a href="#s15">15. Glossary</a></div>
<div class="section-header" id="s1">
  <span class="num">1</span>
  <h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card ci span2">
    <div class="ctitle ci">Segment disclosure as management actually sees the business</div>
    <div class="principle">ASBJ Statement No. 17 requires disclosure of FINANCIAL information about an entity's OPERATING SEGMENTS — organised around a "MANAGEMENT APPROACH": segments are identified based on the entity's OWN INTERNAL organisational structure, the way MANAGEMENT actually allocates resources and evaluates PERFORMANCE — rather than an EXTERNALLY imposed, standardised industry classification. This lets readers see the business through the SAME lens management itself uses.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s2">
  <span class="num">2</span>
  <h2>Scope</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cw span2">
    <div class="ctitle cw">Companies with publicly traded securities preparing consolidated (or individual) financial statements</div>
    <div class="principle">This Statement applies to companies (typically LISTED companies) with PUBLICLY TRADED securities, requiring segment disclosure as part of their FINANCIAL statements.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s3">
  <span class="num">3</span>
  <h2>The Management Approach — Segments as Management Sees Them</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cd span2">
    <div class="ctitle cd">Identified by internal reporting structure, not external classification</div>
    <div class="text-supporting">Operating segments are identified based on COMPONENTS of the entity for which SEPARATE FINANCIAL information is available and REGULARLY REVIEWED by the entity's CHIEF OPERATING DECISION-MAKING body (typically the board of directors or an equivalent senior management group) for the purpose of ALLOCATING resources and ASSESSING performance — a genuinely MANAGEMENT-DRIVEN identification process, MOVING AWAY from the OLDER approach of segmenting purely by RISK-AND-REWARD industry/geographic categories.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s4">
  <span class="num">4</span>
  <h2>Identifying Reportable Segments — the Quantitative Thresholds</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cs span2">
    <div class="ctitle cs">The 10% test, applied across revenue, profit/loss, or assets</div>
    <div class="text-supporting">An operating segment is a "REPORTABLE SEGMENT" requiring separate disclosure if it meets AT LEAST ONE of several QUANTITATIVE thresholds — generally, its REVENUE, its PROFIT OR LOSS (in absolute terms), or its ASSETS represent<strong>10% OR MORE</strong>of the COMBINED total for all operating segments — broadly MIRRORING IFRS 8's own 10% quantitative thresholds internationally.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s5">
  <span class="num">5</span>
  <h2>Required Segment-Level Disclosures</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cd span2">
    <div class="ctitle cd">Revenue, profit/loss, assets, and other measures used by management</div>
    <div class="td-table">
      <div class="th cols-1-2"><span>Disclosure item</span><span>Description</span></div>
      <div class="tr cols-1-2"><span>Segment revenue</span><span>Split between external customers and intersegment transactions</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt cols-1-2"><span>Segment profit or loss</span><span>Measured on the SAME basis management itself uses internally</span></div>
      <div class="tr cols-1-2"><span>Segment assets</span><span>If regularly reported to the chief operating decision-making body</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s6">
  <span class="num">6</span>
  <h2>Reconciliation to Consolidated Totals</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cw span2">
    <div class="ctitle cw">Bridging segment totals back to the consolidated financial statements</div>
    <div class="text-supporting">Because segment measures are based on MANAGEMENT's own internal reporting (which may use DIFFERENT accounting policies or allocation methods than the CONSOLIDATED financial statements), a RECONCILIATION is required, bridging the TOTAL of all reportable segments' revenue, profit/loss, and assets BACK to the corresponding CONSOLIDATED financial statement totals, identifying and explaining any MATERIAL reconciling items.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s7">
  <span class="num">7</span>
  <h2>Entity-Wide Disclosures — Products, Geography, and Major Customers</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cd span2">
    <div class="ctitle cd">Additional disclosures required regardless of segment structure</div>
    <div class="text-supporting">EVEN where an entity has only a SINGLE reportable segment, certain ENTITY-WIDE disclosures are still required: revenue from EXTERNAL customers by MAJOR product/service category; revenue and non-current assets by GEOGRAPHIC area; and information about SIGNIFICANT reliance on any SINGLE external customer (generally where revenue from one customer represents 10% or more of total revenue).</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s8">
  <span class="num">8</span>
  <h2>Worked Example — Applying the 10% Reportable Segment Threshold</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cw span2">
    <div class="ctitle cw">Determining which segments must be separately reported</div>
    <div class="worked worked--flush">
      <div class="wk-title">Example: A company with four operating segments</div>
      <div class="wk-body">A company's four operating segments report revenue of ¥600,000,000, ¥250,000,000, ¥90,000,000, and ¥60,000,000 respectively (total: ¥1,000,000,000).</div>
      <div class="wk-answer">The 10% threshold = ¥100,000,000. Segments 1 (¥600,000,000, 60%) and 2 (¥250,000,000, 25%) EXCEED the threshold and must be SEPARATELY reported. Segments 3 (¥90,000,000, 9%) and 4 (¥60,000,000, 6%) FALL BELOW the threshold — though they may still be VOLUNTARILY disclosed separately, or COMBINED into an "all other segments" category.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s9">
  <span class="num">9</span>
  <h2>Worked Example — a Segment Reconciliation</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card ci span2">
    <div class="ctitle ci">Bridging internal management figures to the consolidated total</div>
    <div class="worked worked--flush">
      <div class="wk-title">Example: Reconciling segment profit to consolidated profit</div>
      <div class="wk-body">The total of all reportable segments' profit is ¥180,000,000. Consolidated profit before tax is ¥165,000,000, with the ¥15,000,000 difference arising from unallocated corporate expenses not attributed to any segment.</div>
      <div class="wk-answer">The company discloses the RECONCILIATION: ¥180,000,000 (total segment profit) LESS ¥15,000,000 (unallocated corporate expenses) =<strong>¥165,000,000</strong>(consolidated profit before tax) — clearly explaining the NATURE of the reconciling item.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s10">
  <span class="num">10</span>
  <h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cd span2">
    <div class="ctitle cd">What ASBJ Statement No. 17 requires</div>
    <div class="check-list"><span>Basis for identifying operating segments and the measurement basis for segment profit/loss</span><span>Segment revenue, profit/loss, assets, and reconciliation to consolidated totals</span><span>Entity-wide disclosures on products, geography, and major customer reliance</span></div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s11">
  <span class="num">11</span>
  <h2>Critical Judgments</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cn span2">
    <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
        <div class="dot-list"><span>Identifying the entity's chief operating decision-making body and its actual internal reporting structure</span><span>Whether to aggregate similar operating segments into a single reportable segment</span></div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
        <div class="dot-list"><span>Generally minimal — this Statement is primarily a disclosure standard reflecting management's own internal figures</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s12">
  <span class="num">12</span>
  <h2>Relationship to IAS/IFRS</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card ci span2">
    <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — J-GAAP vs IFRS 8</div>
    <div class="td-table">
      <div class="th cols-12-12-12"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">J-GAAP (ASBJ Statement No. 17)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IFRS 8)</span></div>
      <div class="tr cols-12-12-12"><span>Segment identification approach</span><span>Management approach</span><span>Management approach — converged</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt cols-12-12-12"><span>Quantitative thresholds</span><span>10% of revenue, profit/loss, or assets</span><span>10% of revenue, profit/loss, or assets — converged</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="note">This is one of the MOST genuinely converged standards in the J-GAAP suite — ASBJ Statement No. 17 was developed AS PART of the joint ASBJ/IASB convergence project SPECIFICALLY to align with IFRS 8's management approach, itself based on the equivalent US GAAP standard.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s13">
  <span class="num">13</span>
  <h2>Statement Mapping</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cs span2">
    <div class="ctitle cs">Where every segment disclosure lands</div>
    <div class="td-table">
      <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
      <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Segment information</span><span>Notes only</span><span>No effect on the primary financial statement figures themselves</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s14">
  <span class="num">14</span>
  <h2>Related Standards</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cd span2">
    <div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
    <div class="td-table">
      <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
      <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>ASBJ Statement No. 22</span><span>Consolidated Financial Statements — supplies the consolidated totals segment figures reconcile to</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s15">
  <span class="num">15</span>
  <h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cn span2">
    <div class="glossary-item">
      <div class="glossary-term">Management approach</div>
      <div class="glossary-def">Identifying operating segments based on an entity's actual internal organisational and reporting structure, as used by its chief operating decision-making body.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="glossary-item glossary-item--last">
      <div class="glossary-term">Reportable segment</div>
      <div class="glossary-def">An operating segment meeting at least one of the quantitative 10% thresholds (revenue, profit/loss, or assets), requiring separate disclosure.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="legend">
  <div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (Japan GAAP terminology)</div>
  <div class="lrow"><span class="badge bs">BS</span>Balance Sheet (貸借対照表)<span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span>Statement of Income / Profit and Loss Statement (損益計算書)<span class="badge bo">Net Assets</span>純資産の部<span class="badge be">SCNA</span>Statement of Changes in Net Assets (株主資本等変動計算書)<span class="badge bc">SCF</span>Statement of Cash Flows (キャッシュ・フロー計算書)</div>
</div>
<div class="footer-note">ASBJ Statement No. 17 — Accounting Standard for Disclosures about Segments of an Enterprise · Extended Reference Poster · Self-contained, no internet connection required</div>
  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
